  "worked_solution": "For a discrete random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}r(x)f(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nr(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{{X}^{2}}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,f(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}}\\cdot 0.3+\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}\\cdot 0.2+\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =16\\cdot 0.3+9\\cdot 0.2+4\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4.8+1.8+2 \\\\  & =8.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
